Recall that for two vectors $\mathbf{u}, \mathbf{v} \in \mathbb{R}^n,$ the **dot product** is defined as the sum of products of corresponding components:

$$
\begin{aligned}u\cdot v & =[\begin{aligned}{u}_{1} \\ {u}_{2} \\ ⋮ \\ {u}_{n}\end{aligned}]\cdot [\begin{aligned}{v}_{1} \\ {v}_{2} \\ ⋮ \\ {v}_{n}\end{aligned}] \\  & ={u}_{1}\cdot {v}_{1}+{u}_{2}\cdot {v}_{2}+⋯+{u}_{n}\cdot {v}_{n} \\  & ={\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{u}_{i}{v}_{i}\end{aligned}
$$

 Note that if we interpret [math]\mathbf{u}[/math] and [math]\mathbf{v}[/math] as [math]n\!\times\! 1[/math] matrices, with the transpose of [math]\mathbf{u}[/math] being a [math]1\!\times\! n[/math] matrix, then the dot product is just matrix multiplication:

\begin{align*}
	 \mathbf{u}\cdot\mathbf{v} =  \mathbf{u}^T\mathbf{v} = \begin{bmatrix} u_1 & u_2 & \cdots & u_n \end{bmatrix}\begin{bmatrix} v_1 \\ v_2 \\ \vdots \\ v_n \end{bmatrix}  

\end{align*}

